    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d666">

                <pb facs="0992" n="864" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg864"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 666.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Cox to Mr. Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-09-24"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 24,
                        1885</date>. (Received October 12.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 22.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 16, of August 29, refers
                    to the proposed expulsion from Safed, Palestine, of two American citizens, viz,
                    Louis Lubrowsky and his brother. They are Hebrews, but, as I took good care to
                    ascertain authentically, they are American citizens.</p>
                <p>Before my audience, I was called on to advise with Mr. Heap, the consul-general,
                    as to this very case. My first concern was the ascertainment of their
                    citizenship. After that, as the inclosures will satisfy you, we were not
                    derelict in anticipating, as it were, your stringent instructions for the
                    protection and vindication of American citizenship in this case.</p>
                <p>The arrest, or rather the bond required of these brothers, was not because they
                    were guilty of crime or otherwise amenable to Turkish law. The simple question
                    and response was, “Are they not Jews? If so, they must go.” We said, “No! unless
                    by superior force.” The telegram we sent made this our conclusion plain. * *
                    *</p>
                <p>No more is heard of the case. That signifies a suspension of the attempt to
                    expel.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I inclose to you the dispatch of Mr. Heap, which is a résumé of this
                    business.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>S. S. COX.</signed></closer>













